{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Caught and fixed a real regression I had introduced — worth recording because I had ALREADY talked myself past it.\n\nThe integration tier failed tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_compact_negative.py::test_compact_all_limit_compacts_only_n. That test seeds four fresh tickets (one CREATE event each) and expects compact-all to backfill them. My implementation selected purely on 'foldable count > threshold', and 1 is not > 10, so compact-all silently STOPPED backfilling small tickets. A one-line behaviour change to an existing documented contract, invisible in the unit tier because every fixture I wrote happened to be over threshold.\n\nWhat makes this worth writing down: my own approved plan said 'a widening, not a replacement' and 'backfill becomes the special case where the whole log is foldable'. That second clause was simply wrong — a 1-event ticket's whole log IS foldable, but 1 is not greater than 10, so the threshold arm does not cover the backfill case. The plan reviewer had pushed me twice on selection semantics and I still got this wrong in the direction the plan itself warned against. The test caught what my reasoning did not.\n\nFix: selection is now explicitly TWO ARMS, either of which selects —\n  1. Backfill (historical, preserved): no SNAPSHOT and at least one foldable event.\n  2. Recurrence (new): foldable count > compact.threshold, whatever the snapshot state.\nArm 2 alone regresses backfill; arm 1 alone is the one-time-only bug this story exists to fix. Both converge: after a fold the ticket has a SNAPSHOT and its live count is back under threshold, so neither arm re-selects it.\n\nAdded two tests for the arm I broke — a small snapshot-less ticket is still backfilled, and the backfill arm converges on the next sweep — and corrected test_under_threshold_ticket_is_not_selected, which had been passing for the wrong reason (it asserted a snapshot-LESS ticket was skipped, which was only true because of the bug). Also corrected docs/concurrency.md I9b, which described only the threshold rule.\n\nProcess note for myself: CI's main pytest invocation excludes the integration tier (-m 'not integration'), and this is the SECOND time in this session that the only real failure lived exactly there — change 1702's s3_doctor flake was the first. Running tests/integration explicitly is not optional here.\n\nGates re-run clean after the fix: lint, mypy (519 files), complexity baseline unchanged, wall-clock-assert, comment-hygiene, docs-index. Both full tiers re-running now."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786607165835747001,"uuid":"cf9d89b2-e646-4cf0-bde3-5693a7fa6687"}